# OvertheWire-Bandit-Levels.
Tips for OTW Bandit challenges
Before reading the solution i recommend you to check the man of nmap and ncat

-------------------------LEVEL16--------------------------
#First you must use nmap to scan the localhost ports from 31000 to 32000.
nmap -A localhost -p 31000-32000

#Then use the command ncat to submit the current password to get the new one.
ncat --ssl localhost "PORT"

#Finally connect to bandit17 from your own machine.
ssh -i "KEY WITH THE PASSWORD" bandit17@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220

-------------------------LEVEL 17-------------------------
#For this level you have to use the command "diff".
diff passwords.new passwords.old
#The output "<" belongs to the first input file and ">" to the last one (you have to choose the password from passwords.new).

-------------------------LEVEL 18-------------------------
#For this level you have to use ssh but with cat at the end.
ssh bandit17@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220 cat "file"

-------------------------LEVEL 19-------------------------
#Now we will cover the setuid. It is a "Temporary Master Key". When a program has the setuid active, it doesn't run with your privileges, instead with the program's privileges.
#An attacker will try to find programs with setuid active to gain root privileges.
./bandit20-do cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit20
#With the command above you can read the file "bandit20" (you did not have permission to do it) with the privileges of bandit20-do.

-------------------------LEVEL 20-------------------------
#For this level you must use nc with the arguments for listen (-l) and port (-p)
#Then you will need 2 terminals connected to the current level. 

#First terminal
./"BINARY_FILE" PORT

#Second terminal
nc -l -p PORT

#Finally print the level's 20 password into the second terminal and ENTER

-------------------------LEVEL 21-------------------------
#In this level you must know what "cron" is. After you learn that, you can continue:
cd /etc/cron.d
#Then when you are in the cron directory make a cat to the cronjob of the next level
cat cronjob_bandit22
#Now make a cat to the /usr/bin/... .sh 
#The cat's output gives you the next level's password hash. You must do a cat again to the directory (tmp/"HASH")
cat /tmp/"HASH"
#The output is the password

-------------------------LEVEL 22-------------------------
#The first 3 steps are the same as LEVEL 21
cd /etc/cron.d
cat cronjob_bandit23
cat /usr/bin/... .sh
#Now you have to make an echo in the terminal changing the variable ($) to bandit23
echo I am user bandit23 | md5sum | cut -d ' ' -f 1
#The output is the password 

-------------------------LEVEL 23------------------------
#Go to /tmp, create a directory for your exploit, and grant it full permissions:
mkdir -p /tmp/my_exploit && chmod 777 /tmp/my_exploit
cd /tmp/my_exploit
#Create a script that reads the bandit24 password and saves it to a file you can access:
-----------
#!/bin/bash
cat /etc/bandit_pass/bandit24 > /tmp/my_exploit/pass.txt
-----------
#Set permissions
chmod 777 script.sh
touch pass.txt && chmod 777 pass.txt

#Copy your script to the directory where the `cron` job executes files:
cp script.sh /var/spool/bandit24/foo/

#Retrieve the password
Wait up to 60 seconds for the `cron` job to run, then check the output:
cat pass.txt

#The output is the password
